# /usr/include/sysexits.h: exit status codes for system programs

Error numbers begin at `EX__BASE` to reduce the possibility of clashing with
other exit statuses that random programs may already return. The meaning of the
codes is approximately as follows:

## EX_OK		0

| number | description            |
| ------ | ---------------------- |
| 0      | successful termination |

## EX__BASE	64

| number | description                   |
| ------ | ----------------------------- |
| 64     | base value for error messages |

## EX_USAGE

| number | description              |
| ------ | ------------------------ |
| 64     | command line usage error |

The command was used incorrectly, e.g., with the wrong number of arguments, a
bad flag, a bad syntax in a parameter, or whatever.

## EX_DATAERR

| number | description       |
| ------ | ----------------- |
| 65     | data format error |

The input data was incorrect in some way. This should only be used for user's
data & not system files.

## EX_NOINPUT

| number | description       |
| ------ | ----------------- |
| 66     | cannot open input |

An input file (not a system file) did not exist or was not readable. This could
also include errors like "No message" to a mailer (if it cared to catch it).

## EX_NOUSER

| number | description       |
| ------ | ----------------- |
| 67     | addressee unknown |

The user specified did not exist. This might be used for mail addresses or
remote logins.

## EX_NOHOST

| number | description       |
| ------ | ----------------- |
| 68     | host name unknown |

The host specified did not exist. This is used in mail addresses or network
requests.

## EX_UNAVAILABLE

| number | description         |
| ------ | ------------------- |
| 69     | service unavailable |

A service is unavailable. This can occur if a support program or file does not
exist. This can also be used as a catchall message when something you wanted to
do doesn't work, but you don't know why.

## EX_SOFTWARE

| number | description             |
| ------ | ----------------------- |
| 70     | internal software error |

An internal software error has been detected. This should be limited to
non-operating system related errors as possible.

## EX_OSERR

| number | description                     |
| ------ | ------------------------------- |
| 71     | system error (e.g., can't fork) |

An operating system error has been detected. This is intended to be used for
such things as "cannot fork", "cannot create pipe", or the like. It includes
things like getuid returning a user that does not exist in the passwd file.

## EX_OSFILE

| number | description              |
| ------ | ------------------------ |
| 72     | critical OS file missing |

Some system file (e.g., /etc/passwd, /etc/utmp, etc.) does not exist, cannot be
opened, or has some sort of error (e.g., syntax error).

## EX_CANTCREAT

| number | description                     |
| ------ | ------------------------------- |
| 73     | can't create (user) output file |

A (user specified) output file cannot be created.

## EX_IOERR

| number | description        |
| ------ | ------------------ |
| 74     | input/output error |

An error occurred while doing I/O on some file.

## EX_TEMPFAIL

| number | description                            |
| ------ | -------------------------------------- |
| 75     | temp failure; user is invited to retry |

temporary failure, indicating something that is not really an error. In
sendmail, this means that a mailer (e.g.) could not create a connection, and the
request should be reattempted later.

## EX_PROTOCOL

| number | description              |
| ------ | ------------------------ |
| 76     | remote error in protocol |

the remote system returned something that was "not possible" during a protocol
exchange.

## EX_NOPERM

| number | description       |
| ------ | ----------------- |
| 77     | permission denied |

You did not have sufficient permission to perform the operation. This is not
intended for file system problems, which should use `NOINPUT` or `CANTCREAT`,
but rather for higher level permissions.

# EX_CONFIG

| number | description         |
| ------ | ------------------- |
| 78     | configuration error |
